WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.129 --> 00:00:12.949
Hey David, are race based dating preferences
and sexual preferences, a form of racism.

00:00:12.949 --> 00:00:18.360
So I'm going to answer this as asked because
there is, you know, sort of the most narrow

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:22.980
answer to a question like this. But it's important
to understand, and I mentioned this before,

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:28.039
very often these types of questions are not
asked in good faith. And I'm not saying Ken

00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:32.009
is not asking the question in good faith,
it's just important to understand how very

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:37.350
often there are people who ask these questions
in order to elicit a particular answer, which

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:45.870
then is used to justify some further bad argument
or bad take. And I don't want to be doing

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:50.840
that. So I'll sort of get to that in a little
bit. So in the most technical sense is something

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:59.510
like I'm a white male. I am not attracted
to Asian women or black women. Is that racism?

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:05.920
If you want to play the linguistic game, strictly
speaking, that is something called sexual

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.400
racism.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:12.200
Now don't be triggered by the word. Okay,
we're trying to have adult conversation here.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.740
Conversations here. We're trying to have conversations
that have context, so don't just go crazy

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:23.030
because I'm calling something racism. This
is actually a deep topic with a lot of nuance

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:29.700
behind it and there's really a lot of sociology
and a lot of things behind this. On the one

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:36.310
hand, people are attracted to certain other
people, oftentimes for reasons that are not

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:43.039
particularly discernible. Sometimes people
have a preference for thinner or heavier,

00:01:43.039 --> 00:01:50.520
uh, of others. Sometimes for certain hair
colors, sometimes complexion, certain personalities,

00:01:50.520 --> 00:02:00.359
uh, assert certain hobbies or religion or
skin color, right? Race is a completely reasonable

00:02:00.359 --> 00:02:08.289
basis where one might have a sort of base
level predisposition in favor of or against.

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:13.560
When it comes to physical attraction. And
in principle, this is no big deal. This is

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:18.550
not evidence of systemic, uh, cultural racism.

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:25.360
This, it's none of those things. Okay. Now
let's go beyond that. There is also a reality

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:33.390
that sometimes those preferences can be culturally
ingrained through discriminatory or racist

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:38.831
messages that are sometimes reinforced, uh,
by society and culture. So for example, some

00:02:38.831 --> 00:02:46.340
countries have cast systems and those cast
systems can pervade so strongly that people

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:53.210
believe that something is merely a casual
preference about physical appearance, for

00:02:53.210 --> 00:03:00.410
example. But it's actually ingrained multi-generationally
by teaching that people of a certain cast

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:06.100
are dirty or toxic or to be avoided or whatever.
And it's important to understand that you

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:13.460
might think, I just happen not to be attracted
to X, and X could be a racial characteristic,

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:17.380
or it could be a physical characteristic of
some other kind or whatever the case may be.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.040
And you may not even have an awareness that
it is something cultural that has been ingrained

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:22.750
that is actually responsible for that.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:28.620
Does that mean that it should be enforced
that even if you don't find certain races

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:32.480
attractive, you should have to date them?
Of course not. Of course not. We're not talking

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:36.680
about that. We're just trying to have a more
nuanced and deep understanding of where some

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:41.850
of these preferences can come from in the
United States to, you know, the cast example

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:48.670
is not super relevant in the U S in the U
S attitudes towards interracial relationships

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:55.231
do affect attitudes about other races sexually,
which can translate to someone thinking, I

00:03:55.231 --> 00:04:00.980
just happen not to be attracted to someone
from a certain race, but it be connected to

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:06.050
historical discrimination and taboos about
interracial marriage, which fortunately are

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:12.760
less prevalent in 2019 than they were 50 years
ago. But still to some degree exist. I know

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:20.810
people who tend to date only outside their
race. I know both men and women that are heterosexual

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:27.660
and I know gay men, all of whom have dating
preferences that are for those who are not

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.880
part of their racial group.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:36.240
Uh, it could be by chance, it could be connected
to something in their upbringing. Who knows?

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:41.290
So that's the analysis. In a literal sense,
sexual racism is a thing. And I apologize

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:47.190
for hitting my microphone there. Uh, is this
the type of systemic racism that is endemic

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:52.330
of, uh, is is, uh, a prototypical of the problems
in society? No, it's not. And I'm not saying

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:58.740
that and that's where I think the broader
analysis comes in. The reality is that making

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:08.260
questions of racism about something like sexual
preferences is very often a distraction sometimes

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:15.520
used in bad faith by people who either want
to create fake drama around dating preferences

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:24.130
or uh, to use acceptance for dating preferences
on the basis of race to justify further segregation.

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:29.410
There is a case that some on the internet
like to make that listen, if it's okay to

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:35.920
only want to date people of certain races,
then what's the big deal with more segregation?

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:41.680
Like why don't we live around people of our
own race. And the next thing you know, you're

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:46.280
talking about something like what Richard
Spencer has proposed, a peaceful segregation,

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:52.210
uh, on the races of base. So it's important.
I'm glad to address these questions as if

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:56.041
they are good faith questions and I believe
with peace and love that Ken was asking it

00:05:56.041 --> 00:06:00.310
in good faith, but I would be doing you a
disservice if I didn't tell you that these

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:07.630
types of questions are often used by bad faith
actors to either trick or induce fruitless

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:13.030
or false debates that are really a distraction
from the issues that as a society we're trying

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:15.050
to solve. If you disagree with me, let me
know. I can't wait to hear from you.

